# PaceTasks

Expo (React Native) + TypeScript task app backed by Supabase. Anonymous
auth first — no login wall, each device gets a stable Supabase user id via
`ensureSession()` in `src/lib/supabase.ts` (single-flight, and it never signs in
anonymously over a stored session that merely failed to refresh); an optional account (Settings)
attaches an email to that same user. The UI is calm and card-based: one
system sans everywhere (hierarchy from weight and size), three color themes
(Sage, Pastel Night, Sunny) each in light and dark (see `src/lib/theme.ts` and
`src/hooks/useTheme.tsx`), and a text-only pill tab bar (see `App.tsx`'s
`PillTabBar`). Design work happens on the long-lived `ui-design-w/Meltem`
branch and reaches `main` by pull request; that branch is never deleted.

## Structure

- `App.tsx` — root: wraps the app in `ThemeProvider`, waits for session (a failed start shows the `src/lib/sessionCopy.ts` wording with a "Try again" button, never developer text), wraps the tabs in `SettingsProvider` and `TasksProvider`, sets up the 3-tab navigator (Tasks / Stats / Settings) behind a custom `PillTabBar` (text-only, no icons).
- `src/screens/` — one file per tab screen. Screens own layout + hook wiring, no direct Supabase calls.
- `src/components/` — presentational pieces reused across screens (AddTaskSheet, TaskItem, FocusSessionModal, EndOfDayCard, WeekHeatmap, DropdownPill, ChoiceChips, Stepper, StatCard, BrainDumpSheet, DoNowSheet, OneThingSheet, RolloverCard, TodayHero, WeekStrip, TodayFilterBar, PickNextTaskSheet, SomedaySheet, UpcomingSheet, TaskActionRow, WeeklyResetSheet, AccountCard, AccountSheet, DataCard, DayPicker, DeleteAccountSheet, TextField, ErrorBanner, Button). No data fetching here.
- `src/hooks/` — `useSession`, `useTasks`, `useSettings` (plus the small shared ones described below: `useSheetHandoff`, `useCountdown`, `useHaptics`). The user can change while the app is open (sign-in, sign-out, account deletion): `TasksProvider` and `SettingsProvider` drop the previous user's data the moment `userId` changes and ignore answers that arrive for an earlier user, and `useSession` starts a missing session again when auth reports `SIGNED_OUT`, when the app returns to the foreground (`reviveSession`) or on the error screen's retry. A screen that returns early while loading must still render its sheets (see `sheets` in `SettingsScreen`), or a sheet showing a result is torn down by the reload. All Supabase reads/writes for a screen go through these, never inline in a component. Tasks are loaded once, by `TasksProvider` (`src/hooks/useTasks.tsx`, mounted in `App.tsx` next to `SettingsProvider`), and every screen reads that one copy through `useTasks()`: never load them per screen (a second fetch and a second Realtime channel). A screen that shows tasks must show a failed load with a retry, never zeros or an empty state (see `StatsScreen`). `TasksProvider` also keeps its list live (Realtime INSERT/UPDATE filtered by user, unfiltered DELETE, silent reload on app foreground) and merges every change through `upsertTask`/`removeTask` so a local write and its Realtime echo never duplicate. Settings are loaded once, by `SettingsProvider` (`src/hooks/useSettings.tsx`, mounted in `App.tsx`), and every screen reads that one copy through `useSettings()`: never load them per screen, because two loads for a new user both try to create the settings row and the loser is left without settings. A screen that needs settings must show a failed load with a retry, never an endless spinner (see `SettingsScreen`).
- Reminders are local notifications planned on the phone, never sent by a server. `planReminders` (`src/lib/reminderPlan.ts`, pure and unit-tested; limits in `REMINDER_POLICY`) turns tasks + settings + the clock into the full list: one silent daily check-in at `reminder_time` only for days with open tasks, and each open task's own reminder (`reminder_minutes_before` before its `scheduled_time`, timing rule in `@domain/reminders`). `useReminders` (called from `TaskListScreen`) re-plans after every task or settings change and when the app returns, and `syncReminders` (`src/lib/notifications.ts`) replaces everything scheduled under `REMINDER_ID_PREFIX`. Wording lives in `src/lib/reminderCopy.ts`. Add a new kind of reminder to the plan, never by scheduling a notification elsewhere; keep them few and quiet.
- Brain Dump UI: `useBrainDump` (hook) drives `BrainDumpSheet` + `BrainDumpCandidateRow` (presentational). All transitions live in the pure reducer `src/lib/brainDumpState.ts` (unit-tested) — add new states/actions there, not in the hook. Closing the sheet discards only a proposal under review (`sessionToDiscardOnClose`; never while committing), and the hook's visit counter drops answers that arrive after the sheet closed. `src/lib/brainDumpApi.ts` is the only caller of the `brain-dump` Edge Function and the commit/discard RPCs; it always sends the phone's time zone (`src/lib/deviceContext.ts`). UI wording for issue codes and summaries lives in `src/lib/brainDumpCopy.ts`; due-day labels in `src/lib/dueLabel.ts` (also `formatDayLabel`, "Mon 12 Oct", for lists of past days).
- Today screen layout: the greeting (`greetingForHour(hour)` in `src/lib/todayCopy.ts`), `TodayHero` (encouraging line from `src/lib/heroCopy.ts`, totals from `src/lib/todayCopy.ts`, the one "Pick my next task" button), `WeekStrip` (Monday–Sunday week from the pure `src/lib/weekStrip.ts`; a later day is a button that opens the Upcoming sheet on it only when it has tasks waiting there (`opensUpcoming`), and its dot counts those, never a `"by"` deadline that is already on Today), the collapsed `RolloverCard` row, then `TodayFilterBar` (All / Quick wins / Due today via `filterTodayTasks` in `src/lib/taskSections.ts`; its filter button holds the groupings). `PickNextTaskSheet` is the chooser behind the hero button: "Decide for me" opens One Thing mode, "Fit my time" opens "What can I do now?". Every number on Today comes from `summarizePending`. The hero's line is worded from One Thing mode's first pick (`useOneThing().firstPick` → `heroStart`), so it never promises a start that "Decide for me" won't offer; a new hero line must be tied to that pick the same way.
- "What can I do now?" (shown to users as "Fit my time"): `useDoNow` (hook: the picked time and energy plus the live suggestions) drives `DoNowSheet` (presentational), which renders rows through the screen's own `renderTaskItem`, so timers and completion behave exactly as on Today (a Focus pill there hands off: the sheet closes, then the session opens). All rules live in the pure `@domain/doNow` (`fitsNow`, `selectDoNowTasks`: filter by time and energy, rank, fill the time) — change ranking there, never in the UI, because One Thing mode and Siri will reuse it. Wording lives in `src/lib/doNowCopy.ts`; energy labels in `src/lib/energy.ts`.
- One Thing mode (shown to users as "Decide for me"): `useOneThing` (hook: the current task, the skipped set, the choosing → confirming → completed phases) drives `OneThingSheet` (presentational). The queue comes from `selectOneThingQueue` in `@domain/doNow`, which shares `rankTasks` with "What can I do now?": overdue and due-today first (oldest first), then priority, nearest upcoming deadline, timing fit, firm before maybe, energy fit, then length. Only that last key differs: longest first to fill a set time, shortest first for One Thing so starting is easy. Wording lives in `src/lib/oneThingCopy.ts`.
- Rollover ("I didn't do it"): `useRollover` (hook: proposals, the user's destination overrides, apply, dismiss-until-tomorrow via `src/lib/rolloverStorage.ts`) drives `RolloverCard` (presentational: one slim "N tasks carried over · Review" row in the Today list's header that expands into the proposals). Rules live in the pure `@domain/rollover` (`isUnfinished`, `proposeRollover`, `rolloverPatch`, thresholds in `ROLLOVER_POLICY`); wording in `src/lib/rolloverCopy.ts`.
- Weekly Reset: `useWeeklyReset` (hook) drives `WeeklyResetSheet` (presentational). Which tasks qualify and what each decision does live in the pure `@domain/weeklyReset` (`selectResetItems`, `resetPatch`, `resetOutcome`, thresholds in `WEEKLY_RESET_POLICY`); the intro → reviewing → summary transitions in the pure `src/lib/weeklyResetState.ts` (unit-tested — the items are a snapshot taken at the start, so decisions never reshuffle the run); wording in `src/lib/weeklyResetCopy.ts`. Move decisions reuse the rollover destinations. "Keep as is" is remembered on the phone (`src/lib/weeklyResetStorage.ts`: the kept task ids with the Monday of their week, cleared with the rollover dismissal when the account is deleted), so a kept task stays out of the reset until the next Monday–Sunday week: `keepResetTask` / `keptIdsThisWeek` / `parseKeptResetTasks` in `@domain/weeklyReset` own that rule (a new week starts the record again, so earlier weeks never pile up) and `selectResetItems` skips the ids it is given. Weeks are named by `startOfWeek` in `@domain/dates`.
- Moving and parking tasks: never build an update object inline. Use the patches in `@domain/taskPatch` (`PARK_PATCH`, `UNPARK_PATCH`, `reschedulePatch`) with `useTasks().update` / `updateMany` → `tasksApi.updateTask`. Parking clears the due date, so a task brought back is never instantly overdue.
- Someday: `selectSomedayTasks` (`@domain/todayTasks`) feeds `SomedaySheet`; tasks are parked from `TaskItem`'s menu (`onPark`).
- Upcoming: `selectUpcomingTasks` + `groupUpcomingByDay` (`@domain/todayTasks`) feed `UpcomingSheet`, the only place a task set for one exact later day can be seen before that day ("Move to Today" uses `reschedulePatch`). It opens from the "Upcoming · N" link at the foot of Today and from the week strip. Wording lives in `src/lib/upcomingCopy.ts`. `isUpcoming` is the exact opposite of `isPendingToday` for pending tasks, so no pending task is ever in neither list.
- `src/components/TaskActionRow.tsx` — the row both of those sheets use for a task that is off Today: one pill back onto Today and a trash. Deleting a task for good outside the Today list always asks first through `confirmDestructive` (`src/lib/confirmDestructive.ts`: a native alert with a red button, the browser's own confirm on the web demo, where `Alert.alert` does nothing) with the wording from `src/lib/taskActionsCopy.ts`.
- Accounts are passwordless and optional. `useAccount(session)` drives `AccountCard` (in Settings) and `AccountSheet`; transitions live in the pure reducer `src/lib/accountState.ts` (unit-tested), validation in `@domain/account`, wording in `src/lib/accountCopy.ts`. `src/lib/accountApi.ts` is the only caller of `supabase.auth.updateUser / signInWithOtp / verifyOtp / signOut` (always `{ scope: "local" }`, so other devices stay signed in; a sign-out whose request fails offline still counts as signed out, and the session-failed screen covers the fresh session that cannot start yet), of the `profiles` table and of the `delete_account` function. Sign-up = `updateUser({ email, data })` on the anonymous user + `verifyOtp(type "email_change")`, so the user id (and every task) is kept; never create a second user for sign-up. Sign-up details ride in `user_metadata` so `useAccount` can create the missing `profiles` row on a later start.
- Deleting the account: `DataCard` (Settings, "Your data") opens `DeleteAccountSheet`; `useAccount` exposes `deletion` / `openDeletion` / `confirmDeletion` / `closeDeletion`, with transitions in the pure reducer `src/lib/accountDeletionState.ts` (unit-tested) and wording in `accountCopy.ts`. The sheet remembers the user it asked about and closes if the session changes, so it can never delete a different user. `accountApi.deleteAccount()` calls the `delete_account` database function (deletes the caller's `auth.users` row; every user table cascades from it), then starts a fresh anonymous session. It works with or without a signed-up account. A new table holding user data must reference `auth.users (id) on delete cascade`, and gets a line in `supabase/tests/database/delete_account.test.sql`.
- Public pages live in `docs/` (GitHub Pages): `privacy.html`, `support.html`, `index.html`, sharing `site.css` (the Sunny theme's colours with the app's token names, light and dark, and the self-hosted Archivo in `docs/fonts/`: never link a font or script CDN, the privacy policy promises no third parties). The landing page adds `home.css` and `home.js` ("Fit my time" with simplified `@domain/doNow` rules on the web demo's seeded tasks); its phone screens in `docs/screens/` are filmed from the web demo by `npm run site:screens` (`design/site/buildSiteScreens.mjs`). A feature change that alters how something is used updates `support.html` and the landing page in the same change. Their addresses are defined once in `src/lib/links.ts`; open them with `openExternalLink`. Any change to what is stored about a user (a new table or column, an AI provider, analytics) must update `docs/privacy.html` in the same change.
- `profiles` holds first name, last name and marketing consent only; the email lives in `auth.users`. `marketing_opt_in` must never default to or be pre-set to true, and `marketing_opt_in_at` is owned by the `profiles_stamp` trigger.
- Opening one sheet from another (the add sheet's microphone → Brain Dump, "Pick my next task" → its two helpers, a Focus pill inside "Fit my time" → the Focus session) goes through `useSheetHandoff` (`src/hooks/useSheetHandoff.ts`): the first sheet closes, passes `onDismissed` to its `Modal`'s `onDismiss`, and the next opens only then. Never open the next sheet on a fixed delay: iOS silently shows nothing if the previous one is still animating away.
- `src/components/TextField.tsx` — the labelled form input with its error line; use it for any new form field.
- `src/components/Button.tsx` — the full-width pill button for every sheet (`variant="primary"` for the one main action, `"secondary"` for the calmer alternatives, `"destructive"` for deleting). Don't hand-roll another button style in a sheet.
- `useTasks` mutations (`create`, `complete`, `update`, `updateMany`, `remove`) never throw: they go through its `run` helper, which reports a failure through `error` and resolves `false` (`create` resolves the created task, or `undefined` on failure, so the add sheet can offer Undo). Add new mutations the same way.
- `src/lib/tasksApi.ts` / `src/lib/settingsApi.ts` — the only files that call Supabase directly (besides `brainDumpApi.ts` for Brain Dump). Tasks are created only via `createTask(draft)` → `rpc("create_task")`; never insert into `tasks` directly, since Brain Dump and Siri share that one creation path.
- `supabase/functions/_shared/domain/` — pure, dependency-free TypeScript shared by the app (imported as `@domain/...`) and Edge Functions (Deno). `task.ts` (task model, enums, limits, `TaskDraft`), `dates.ts` (local date keys — "today" always comes from the phone; `startOfWeek` is the Monday of a Monday–Sunday week), `todayTasks.ts` (what the Today list/Siri show), `doNow.ts` ("What can I do now?" rules), `timing.ts` (`timingForHour`: an hour → before/after-work bucket), `rollover.ts` (where unfinished tasks go), `weeklyReset.ts` (which tasks need a weekly decision), `taskPatch.ts` (the only ways a task is moved or parked), `taskCollection.ts`, `taskHistory.ts`. Relative imports inside it must use explicit `.ts` extensions and `import type` for types, or Deno breaks. Test fixtures live in `domain/testing/`.
- `src/lib/paceInsights.ts` — pure functions only: `computePaceInsights` ("My Pace": best hours, strongest weekday, estimate bias; thresholds in `PACE_INSIGHTS_POLICY`). Sentences come from `src/lib/paceInsightsCopy.ts`; `PaceInsightsCard` on the Stats tab only renders them. Add new insights here, each with a minimum amount of history before it speaks.
- `src/lib/stats.ts` — pure functions only (no React, no network): `computeStats` (streaks/accuracy; the current streak counts back from yesterday until today has a task done, and accuracy treats an estimate that was too long like one that was too short) and `buildWeekdayHeatmap` (the Stats tab's Mon-Fri × N-week grid).
- `@domain/taskHistory` — pure functions only, groups tasks by normalized (trimmed, lowercased) title. Powers the add sheet's autocomplete and auto-fill (time, timing, category, energy), and `TaskItem`'s range-timer bounds (`findExactMatch` looked up per task in `TaskListScreen`).
- `src/lib/categories.ts` — fixed list of task categories (`id`, `label`, `icon`, `color`). A task's `category` field stores a category `id` or `null` ("No category"). Add new categories here, never inline in a component.
- `src/components/DropdownPill.tsx` — generic one-tap choice control (Modal-based) used for the per-task destination in `RolloverCard`. Reuse it for any compact, secondary "pick one of a few options" control inside a row.
- `src/components/ChoiceChips.tsx` — a row of always-visible, single-tap chips. Use it only where the choice is the screen's main question (time and energy in `DoNowSheet`); everywhere else prefer `DropdownPill`.
- Countdowns: `useCountdown` (`src/hooks/useCountdown.ts`, math in the pure, tested `src/lib/countdown.ts`) is the only timer. It stores when the countdown ends and reads the time left from the clock on every tick and when the app returns, because iOS runs no JS timers while the phone is locked. Never count seconds down with `setInterval` and `prev - 1`.
- Vibrations: `src/lib/haptics.ts` is the only caller of `expo-haptics`; components ask for a cue through `useHaptics().play(cue)`, which checks the cue's setting (`taskCompleted` → `haptics_enabled`, `timerEnded` → `timer_chime_enabled`). The timer's cue is a buzz, not a sound, and Settings says so (`src/lib/settingsCopy.ts`); a new cue gets its setting in `CUE_SETTING`. One cue per moment: the completion tap plays only after the save succeeded (`completeTask` in `TaskListScreen`), and not when a Focus timer ending already buzzed.
- Times of day are shown in 24-hour form everywhere through `formatTimeOfDay` (`src/lib/timeFormat.ts`).
- `src/components/TaskItem.tsx` — owns two independent timers: a manual actual-minutes stepper (always available via the checkbox; it opens on a line of its own with Cancel and Done) and, for tasks without a `scheduled_time`, a lightweight range timer (tap the minutes pill) that counts down from `suggestedMax` and flags "in range" once past `suggestedMin` — both props computed by the caller (`TaskListScreen`) from `taskHistory`, not fetched inside the component.
- `src/components/FocusSessionModal.tsx` — the Pomodoro countdown (also `useCountdown`), opened from a task's FOCUS badge (shown when `task.scheduled_time` is set — mutually exclusive with the range timer). Reaching zero calls `onComplete` with `actual_minutes = workMinutes` — don't change that without considering the "estimate accuracy" stat, which depends on real actual-vs-estimated data.
- `src/lib/theme.ts` — single source of colors/spacing/radius/typography: `buildTheme(scheme, accentId, paletteId)` returns the active `AppTheme`: the chosen color theme's light/dark base colors, its accent (only the Sage theme takes the user's accent pick; see `paletteUsesAccent`) and `theme.hero`, the colors for the Today hero card and week strip. `theme.fonts.display` and `theme.fonts.mono` both point at the system sans; number styles add `fontVariant: ["tabular-nums"]` so digits line up. Corners use `theme.radius` by role (sm tags, md rows and tiles, lg cards and sheets, pill buttons). There is no static `theme` export. Never hardcode a hex color in a component; add it to the palettes instead (text on an accent fill is `theme.colors.onAccent`, a scrim is `theme.colors.overlay`).
- `src/hooks/useTheme.tsx` — `ThemeProvider`, `useTheme()` (the active theme + appearance prefs) and `makeStyles((theme) => ({...}))`. Every component declares `const useStyles = makeStyles(...)` at the bottom and calls `const styles = useStyles()` inside; never use a module-level `StyleSheet.create` with colors, or the component won't follow light/dark and the accent.
- `src/lib/appearance.ts` (+ `appearanceStorage.ts`) — look & layout prefs saved on the device: theme mode, color theme (`palette`), accent, how Today is grouped, the quick-win limit. `src/lib/taskSections.ts` groups Today's tasks for the chosen layout; `src/lib/taskRhythm.ts` learns how often repeat tasks get done and feeds the add sheet's one-tap "usuals"; `src/lib/dueOptions.ts` turns the add sheet's `DueChoice` (one exact day, or a flexible window: any day / this week / next week) into `due_date`/`due_kind` (`resolveDueChoice`): an exact day is `"on"`, a week is a `"by"` deadline on its Sunday. `src/lib/dayPicker.ts` is the pure calendar math (seven-day strip, Monday-first month grid) behind `DayPicker`, the presentational "When?" control.
- `src/components/AddTaskSheet.tsx` — the only place a single task is typed in: opens on the usuals (one tap adds, with Undo), and typing something new asks what / how long / when, with category, energy, time of day and a fixed start behind one link. Fields the user set by hand are never overwritten by task memory.
- `supabase/functions/_shared/domain/brainDump/` — Brain Dump contract and rules, pure: `aiResult.ts` (zod schema the AI must satisfy; dates are *described* by the model, never computed), `resolveDate.ts`, `normalize.ts` (AI proposal → `TaskDraft`s using history, work hours, categories), `reviewPolicy.ts`, `types.ts` (API response). Category ids come from `@domain/categories`; `src/lib/categories.ts` only adds visuals.
- `supabase/functions/_shared/server/` — Deno-only (never imported by the app): `ai/` (vendor-neutral `LLMClient` + Anthropic/OpenAI adapters, `AIProvider` feature interface, `providerFactory.ts` — the one place that turns secrets into an `AIProvider`: `AI_PROVIDER=rules` gives `rulesAIProvider.ts`, the free rule-based parser, anything else a configured vendor — versioned prompts — bump the prompt version on any prompt change), `brainDump/` (request schema, repository interface + Supabase impl, service, HTTP handler), `http/apiError.ts`. Tests use `node:assert/strict` + `Deno.test` (`deno task test` in `supabase/functions`).
- Rule-based Brain Dump: `@domain/brainDump/rulesParser` (`parseBrainDumpWithRules`) is pure and returns the same `BrainDumpAiResult` a model must, so normalize/review/commit don't know the difference. It powers free mode and the web demo. Every word it knows (weekdays, months, verbs, category words per language) lives in `rulesLexicon.ts`: add vocabulary there, not as a new regex in the parser. Its word patterns use `words()` (Unicode-aware boundaries; plain `\b` breaks on "venerdì"/"yarın"). After changing a rule, bump `RULES_PARSER_VERSION`, add a case to `rulesParser.test.ts` and run `deno task eval --provider rules`.
- Demo code (`src/lib/demo/`) is loaded with `require` only when `isDemoMode` (see `src/lib/supabase.ts`), so a real build never runs it. Don't import anything from `src/lib/demo/` statically outside that folder.
- AI rule: AI output is a proposal. It is schema-validated and normalized, and tasks are only ever created via `create_task` (directly or through `commit_brain_dump`). AI never writes to the database, never picks ids or computes dates. API keys only live in Supabase secrets.
- `supabase/functions/_eval/` — evaluation set and runner for choosing/regressing AI models (`deno task eval`); not deployed.
- `supabase/migrations/` — source of truth for the DB schema, RLS policies and `public.create_task`. Never edit an applied migration; add a new timestamped file and mirror column/enum changes in `@domain/task`. DB tests live in `supabase/tests/database/` (pgTAP).
- Tests: `npm test` (Jest, pinned to Europe/Rome via `jest.globalSetup.js`), `npm run typecheck`, `npm run db:test`.
- Simulated journey: `npm run e2e` exports the web demo and plays it in a headless browser at iPhone size with a fixed clock, writing a film and step screenshots to `e2e/output/` (`npm run verify` = typecheck + Jest + journey; GitHub runs the same on every pull request). `e2e/harness.mjs` owns the browser, server, film and step runner; a journey file (`e2e/todayJourney.mjs`) only lists steps. Find elements by `accessibilityLabel` or visible wording, never by generated class names, and when a change renames a label or button the journey uses, update the journey in the same change.

## Conventions

- Naming: `camelCase` for functions/variables, `PascalCase` for components/types, snake_case only for DB columns (matches Postgres/Supabase convention) — mirrored exactly in `@domain/task` (`Task`, `TaskDraft`) and re-exported from `src/lib/types.ts`.
- Every exported function/component gets a 1–2 sentence comment explaining *why*, not a restatement of the code.
- Timing values are always one of `"before_work" | "after_work" | "anytime"` (see `TaskTiming` in `@domain/task`) — don't introduce new string literals for this (or for priority, energy, due kind, source) without updating the type and the DB check constraint in a new migration.
- Dates: a task's `due_date` is a local calendar day (`"YYYY-MM-DD"`). Compute "today" with `toLocalDateKey(new Date())` from `@domain/dates` — never `toISOString().slice(0, 10)`, which is UTC.
- What shows on Today is decided only by `isPendingToday` (`@domain/todayTasks`): undated tasks, tasks due today or overdue, and every task with a deadline (`due_kind: "by"`), since it can be done any day before it. A task set for one exact later day (`"on"`) is hidden until that day and listed in the Upcoming sheet meanwhile.
- Task status is `"pending" | "someday" | "done"`. `"someday"` tasks are parked: every daily list goes through `isPendingToday`, which only accepts `"pending"`, so they stay hidden without extra filtering.
- `postponed_count` is owned by the `tasks_track_postponement` database trigger (it adds one when an unfinished task's `due_date` moves later, and clears it when a task comes back from `"someday"`). Never write it from the app; to postpone a task, just update its `due_date`.
- Fallbacks for settings that are still loading come from `DEFAULT_SETTINGS` in `src/lib/settingsApi.ts`, never an inline number.
- Category values are a category `id` from `src/lib/categories.ts` or `null` — always look categories up via `getCategory(id)` (handles `null` and unknown/deleted ids) rather than indexing `CATEGORIES` directly.
- A task's `scheduled_time` (`"HH:MM"` or `null`) is what enables Focus mode instead of the range timer — it's independent of `timing`; a task can have both a timing bucket and a fixed time.
- Keep screens dumb: fetching/mutation logic belongs in `src/hooks/*` or `src/lib/*Api.ts`, not inside a screen component.
- Icons: use `Ionicons` from `@expo/vector-icons`. The current design mostly avoids icons in favor of plain text (pills, tab bar) — only add an icon where the design shows one.
- Color: each color theme uses one accent-based palette everywhere (no per-timing color coding on task rows); the fixed brand colors in `PALETTE` are only for expressive surfaces such as the Today hero — category tags are the one place distinct accent hues (`theme.colors.indigo/purple/warning/pink`) are used, to tell categories apart at a glance.
- A task title can be 200 characters. Wherever one is shown large and centred (One Thing, Weekly Reset, Focus) it gets `numberOfLines` with `adjustsFontSizeToFit` and `minimumFontScale`, so it never runs into the header or the buttons.
- Every `Switch` and icon-only control gets an `accessibilityLabel` (for a switch, the title shown beside it).
- Completed tasks are the history behind Pace (streaks, heatmap, My Pace, the usuals, timer ranges): never offer to bulk-delete them. Today already shows only what was finished today.
